Sinosauropteryx, found in China, had a ginger tail with pale stripes, a darker back and a bandit-like mask around its eyes. Anchiornis, a small feathered dinosaur, had grey and black body feathers, black-and-white wings and a reddish crest, like a woodpecker.

Microraptor, the four-winged glider, was glossy black with a shimmer, like a crow or starling. That shine comes from melanosomes lined up in neat layers. It probably helped with showing off, as [[@crow-smart|crow feathers]] catch the light today.

{{story:tiger-stripes}}

The surprise is how slowly most dinosaurs moved. The great majority of trackways show walking speeds of about 3–8 km/h, the pace of a person out for a walk. That makes sense. Animals spend most of their time walking, and only sprint for a few seconds at a time.

At Dinosaur Valley State Park in Glen Rose, Texas, you can see tracks left by long-necked giants and large meat-eaters along the Paluxy River. They walked across soft mud by an ancient shoreline, slowly and steadily.

{{story:fastest-animal}}

To understand Mars, start at home. Sunlight looks white, but it is a mix of all the colours of the rainbow. When it passes through Earth's air, tiny gas molecules bounce blue light around much more than red light. That scattered blue comes at you from every direction, so the whole [[@sky-blue|sky looks blue]].

At sunset, the light has to travel through much more air to reach you. Most of the blue gets scattered away before it arrives. What's left is orange and red, which is why [[@colour-of-sky-sunset|Earth's sunsets glow red]].

{{story:sky-blue}}

ఆక్టోపస్‌కు సుమారు 50 కోట్ల [[neuron|నాడీ కణాలు]] ఉంటాయి. వెన్నెముక లేని జంతువులన్నిటిలో ఇదే ఎక్కువ. కానీ వాటిలో దాదాపు మూడింట రెండు వంతులు తలలో కాదు, చేతుల్లో ఉంటాయి. ప్రతి చేయీ తనంతట తానే తడిమి చూడగలదు, పట్టుకోగలదు, రుచి కూడా చూడగలదు. ఎందుకంటే దాని సక్కర్లలో రుచిని గుర్తించే సెన్సర్లు ఉంటాయి. ప్రధాన మెదడు వేరే ఏదో ఆలోచిస్తున్నప్పుడే, ఒక చేయి రాతి పగులులో వెతికి ఏం పట్టుకోవాలో నిర్ణయించుకోగలదు.

ఆక్టోపస్‌కు [[@what-if-no-bones|ఎముకలే లేవు]]. చిలక ముక్కు లాంటి గట్టి ముక్కు ఒక్కటే ఉంటుంది. అందుకే తన ముక్కు కంటే పెద్దగా ఉన్న ఏ సందులోంచైనా అది దూరిపోగలదు. తప్పించుకోవడంలో దానికి అంత నైపుణ్యం ఎందుకుందో దీంతో కొంత అర్థమవుతుంది.

{{story:brain-works}}

For centuries in Europe, people greeted others by bowing, curtsying or tipping a hat, depending on who was more important. In the 1600s, the Quakers, a Christian group in England, believed that everyone was equal before God. They refused to bow to anyone and shook hands instead.

The Quakers helped spread handshakes in Britain and in American colonies like Pennsylvania. By the 1800s, books on manners described the handshake as a normal greeting between gentlemen. Over time, it became common for everyone, in business, sport and politics.

{{story:manners-world}}

Soldiers belong to regiments, many with histories going back centuries. The Madras Regiment, the oldest infantry regiment, traces its roots to the 1750s. Others include the Sikh Regiment, the Gorkha Rifles, the Rajputana Rifles and the Assam Regiment.

Each has its own traditions, songs and battle cry. The Gorkhas shout "Ayo Gorkhali" ("the Gorkhas are here"). Sikh regiments cry "Jo Bole So Nihal". These traditions help soldiers feel like a family, which matters when they are far from their own.

{{story:donkeys-mules}}

The rule is far older than its name. A clay tablet from [[@babylon|Babylon]], now known as Plimpton 322 and kept at Columbia University in New York, dates from about 1800 BCE. It records numbers from triangles that fit the rule, such as one with sides 119, 120 and 169.

In India, the Sulba Sutras, written to guide the building of fire altars, state the rule clearly. The oldest, by [[@baudhayana|Baudhayana]], is usually dated somewhere between about 800 and 500 BCE. Chinese mathematicians also knew it and called it the *gougu* rule. The Greeks, especially [[@euclid|Euclid]] around 300 BCE, became famous for writing down careful proofs.

{{story:ancient-greece}}

After the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]], plastic was a new and exciting material. In 1947 Christiansen bought a plastic injection-moulding machine, which squirts hot liquid plastic into a metal mould.

In 1949 the company released Automatic Binding Bricks, based on Kiddicraft self-locking bricks invented in England by Hilary Fisher Page. They had studs on top but were hollow underneath, so models fell apart easily. Many shops thought plastic toys would never replace wooden ones.

{{story:plastic-made}}

In the late 1980s and 1990s, engineers at the Fraunhofer Institute in Germany developed the MP3 format. They used what scientists know about [[@ears-hear|hearing]]. Very quiet sounds next to loud ones get masked, so we barely notice them. Very high sounds are hard for many adults to hear at all.

MP3 and later formats remove much of this hidden information. The file becomes around ten times smaller, and most listeners can't tell the difference on everyday [[@sound-recording|headphones]]. Streaming services use similar methods and switch to lower quality automatically when your connection is weak.

{{story:binary-code}}

Ayurveda's classical texts, the *Charaka Samhita* and the *Sushruta Samhita*, were written down roughly 2,000 years ago, though they were revised over centuries. The second is famous for its surgery, which you can read about in [[@sushruta|Sushruta's story]].

Ayurveda teaches that the body is governed by three *doshas*, called vata, pitta and kapha, which are linked to qualities like movement, heat and stability. Illness is seen as these falling out of balance, and treatment tries to restore it. Many Indian kitchen habits, such as adding [[@turmeric|turmeric]] and ginger to food, are tied to Ayurvedic ideas.

{{story:turmeric}}

Many bilingual people mix languages in the same sentence, like "Mujhe homework finish karna hai". This is called code-switching. It isn't a mistake. It's often a skilled choice, picking the word that fits best or signalling which group you belong to.

In India, mixing is everywhere, from Hinglish adverts to Tanglish film songs. In the United States, many families blend English with [[@spanish-language|Spanish]], Chinese or Hindi. About one American in five speaks a language other than English at home.

{{story:languages-india}}

Some nerve signals travel more than 100 metres a second, quicker than India's fastest trains. That's how a [[@reflexes|reflex]] can jerk your hand off a hot pan before you even feel the [[@pain|pain]]. The signal goes to your spinal cord and straight back to your muscles, and the brain is told afterwards.

The signals are real [[@electricity-made|electricity]], though far weaker than the current in a wall socket. Doctors can measure them with [[@future-brain-computer|sensors on the scalp]]. The wavy lines they record, called brain waves, change when you are awake, drowsy or [[@body-sleepwalking|deeply asleep]].

{{story:ai-what}}

Whirl a stone on a string and the string goes tight. The stone wants to fly off in a straight line, and the string keeps pulling it back. A space elevator works the same way. The Earth spins, the counterweight beyond geostationary orbit is flung outwards, and the cable between them stays taut.

[[@gravity-fall|Gravity]] pulls the lower part of the cable down. The counterweight pulls the upper part out. As long as they balance, the cable hangs like a plumb line, straight up from the equator. It would be in tension, like a rope in tug of war, rather than squashed like a pillar.

{{story:elevators}}

Inside almost every game is a short set of steps that repeats very fast. Programmers call it the game loop. First, the game checks what you pressed. Next, it updates the world: the car moves forward a little, the enemy turns, a coin spins. Then it draws everything on the screen.

That loop usually runs 30 to 60 times a second. Each picture it draws is called a frame, made of millions of tiny coloured dots called [[@pixels|pixels]]. Because the frames change so quickly, your brain sees smooth movement, the same trick that makes cartoons work.

{{story:animation-how}}

Every year, from about June to September, the Nile flooded. When the water went down, it left a layer of black, [[fertile|fertile]] mud. Egyptians called their country Kemet, "the black land", after this soil. The desert beyond was Deshret, "the red land".

Life followed three seasons. Akhet was the flood, when farmers could not work their fields and some joined building projects. Peret was the growing season. Shemu was harvest time. Officials measured the height of the flood each year, because it told them how big the harvest, and the taxes, would be.

{{story:egypt-nile}}

Uruk stood beside the Euphrates in what is now southern Iraq. By around 3000 BCE, it may have held tens of thousands of people. It had temples on raised platforms, workshops, and a city wall later said to be about 9 km long.

Running a place this big meant tracking grain, workers and taxes. Officials began pressing marks into clay tablets. Those marks grew into [[@writing-began|writing]], one of the first systems in the world. Uruk was one of many city-states in [[@mesopotamia|Mesopotamia]], each with its own ruler and god.

{{story:indus-cities}}

Forgeries are a real problem. In 1983, the German magazine *Stern* announced it had found Hitler's secret diaries. Experts tested the paper and found a whitening chemical that wasn't used until after the war. The glue and threads were modern too. The diaries had been forged by a dealer in fake war souvenirs.

Checking where and when something was made, from its paper to its [[@words-handwriting|handwriting]] to its words, is the same habit people now need to spot [[@fake-news|fake news]] online.

{{story:fake-news}}

In 1659, Bijapur sent a powerful general, Afzal Khan, to crush him. The two met for talks below Pratapgad fort. Each side later said the other struck first. Shivaji, who wore hidden armour and a weapon called *bagh nakh*, steel tiger claws, killed Afzal Khan, and his forces routed the enemy army.

In 1663, the Mughal general Shaista Khan had camped in Pune, in Shivaji's own childhood home. Shivaji and a small band slipped in at night, disguised as a wedding party according to some accounts. Shaista Khan escaped but lost fingers, and the Mughals were badly embarrassed.

{{story:unesco-sites}}

South of the mountains, rivers have spread mud and silt over millions of years, building some of the flattest land on Earth. The Indo-Gangetic plain runs from Punjab to West Bengal and Assam.

The soil here is deep and rich, so [[@people-farmers-day|farmers grow wheat]], rice and sugarcane. Hundreds of millions of people live on these plains, in cities like Delhi, Lucknow, Patna and Kolkata. Where the Ganga and Brahmaputra reach the sea, they form the Sundarbans delta, the largest mangrove forest in the world.

{{story:monsoon}}

The site at Prayagraj is called the Sangam, meaning confluence. During the [[monsoon|monsoon]], much of it lies underwater. Only when the rivers shrink in winter does the land appear, so building starts each autumn.

Engineers float pontoon bridges across the river, lay steel plates over sand for roads and set up electricity for lights and loudspeakers. In 2025 the authorities said they put up about 1.5 lakh toilets. Water was piped to taps across the site, and tens of thousands of police and volunteers managed the crowds. It is [[@urban-planning|urban planning]] on fast-forward.

{{story:urban-planning}}

The Golden Gate is a suspension bridge. Two tall towers hold up two giant cables, and the road hangs from those cables on thinner steel ropes. Each main cable is about 92 cm thick and made of over 27,000 thin wires bundled together. Laid end to end, the wires in both cables would wrap around Earth more than three times.

When it opened, its main span of about 1,280 m was the longest suspension span in the world. Workers wore hard hats, and a safety net under the deck saved 19 men during building. Even so, 11 workers died.

{{story:bridges}}

Tagore's songs, called [[@rabindra-sangeet|Rabindra Sangeet]], number more than 2,000. Bengalis sing them at weddings, festivals and in quiet moments. His short story Kabuliwala, about a fruit-seller from Afghanistan and a little Calcutta girl who becomes his friend, has been filmed several times.

He began painting seriously when he was in his sixties. He started by turning crossings-out in his [[manuscript|manuscripts]] into strange creatures and faces. He went on to make more than 2,000 paintings and drawings, and they were shown in Paris, London and New York.

{{story:anthems}}

The samosa appears in records from [[medieval|medieval]] India. The poet Amir Khusrau, who lived in Delhi around 1300, mentioned princes enjoying samosas made of meat, ghee and onion.

The [[@morocco|Moroccan]] traveller [[@ibn-battuta|Ibn Battuta]] visited the court of Sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq in the 1330s. He described a pastry called sambusak, filled with minced meat, almonds, pistachios, walnuts and spices, served at royal feasts. In the 1500s, the *Ain-i-Akbari*, a record of [[@akbar|Akbar]]'s court, gave a recipe for a similar dish.

{{story:ibn-battuta}}

In 1767, the English scientist Joseph Priestley lived next door to a brewery in Leeds. Beer gives off carbon dioxide as it ferments, and a layer of the gas lay over the vats. Priestley hung a bowl of water above it and found the water picked up a pleasant, fizzy taste.

He later worked out how to make carbonated water at home and published his method in 1772. A few years later, in 1783, a watchmaker named Johann Jacob Schweppe started making soda water to sell in Geneva. The company named after him still exists.

{{story:bubbles-round}}
